    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In this paper we test a hypothesis that local stress rotation may be responsible for sequential shearing of splay fractures to form apparent conjugate fault arrays in two fault sets with the opposite sense of shearing and a dihedral intersection angle between them. Numerical analyses using both eXtended Finite Element and Boundary Element methods land support for the hypothesis: They both show that propagation increments of the splay fractures rotate about 770 with respect to the original fracture orientation as the splay fracture tips extend away from the fault tips. Narrower distance between the initial sub parallel sheared fractures and a moderate amount of stretching (extension) or contraction (in x-direction perpendicular to the original array) enhance this rotation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Liu, Fushen, Aydin, Atilla and Borja, Ronaldo. (2009). Stress or Fracture Path Rotation? Implications for Fault Network Development. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/zv223qw3364",
            "type": "preferred citation",
